      "id": "p0258-b014",
      "kind": "aside",
      "source": {
        "text": "On a mostly unrelated topic, a rare practice in localization is something I call \"reverse localization\". As an example, let's pretend you're translating a Japanese game into English. But instead of translating like normal, imagine that what you're looking at is actually a Japanese translation of something that was originally in English. It's then your job to get as close to that \"original\" text as possible. Naturally, this requires an incredible amount of experience and skill to accomplish successfully.",
        "raw": "On a mostly unrelated topic, a rare practice in localization is something I\ncall \"reverse localization\". As an example, let's pretend you're translating a\nJapanese game into English. But instead of translating like normal, imagine\nthat what you're looking at is actually a Japanese translation of something\nthat was originally in English. It's then your job to get as close to that\n\"original\" text as possible. Naturally, this requires an incredible amount of\nexperience and skill to accomplish successfully.",
